# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Luke 17 opens with Jesus speaking to a named company: “Then said he unto the disciples, It is impossible but that offences will come: but woe unto him, through whom they come!” (17:1). Luke does not name the place in 17:1. The chapter before it has Jesus speaking “also unto his disciples” (16:1), and then names a second company: “And the Pharisees also, who were covetous, heard all these things: and they derided him” (16:14). Luke later says that Jesus “was demanded of the Pharisees, when the kingdom of God should come” (17:20), and that He was on the way “to Jerusalem” (17:11). Luke does not say that the Pharisees of 16:14 are present at 17:1.'))
A(('p', '2.2 What Jesus says before the story. Before 17:7 Luke gives four sayings in a row. Jesus speaks of offences and of one who offends “one of these little ones” (17:1–2). He then says, “Take heed to yourselves: If thy brother trespass against thee, rebuke him; and if he repent, forgive him” (17:3), and adds that if the brother trespasses seven times in a day and seven times turns again, saying “I repent,” he is to be forgiven (17:4). These sayings are set out in the table below. The story does not repeat any of their words.'))
A(('call', 'A Request Stands Before the Story',
   'The apostles said unto the Lord, “Increase our faith” (Luke 17:5). The Lord answered with a saying about a grain of mustard seed and a sycamine tree (17:6), and the next verse begins, “But which of you” (17:7). Luke does not call 17:7–10 a parable, does not say that it answers the request, and does not say what the word “But” sets against what comes before. Nothing is added here to make it do so.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The request and the answer. “And the apostles said unto the Lord, Increase our faith” (17:5). Luke’s word for the speakers here is “the apostles,” and in 17:1 it is “the disciples.” Luke does not say that these are two companies or one. “And the Lord said, If ye had faith as a grain of mustard seed, ye might say unto this sycamine tree, Be thou plucked up by the root, and be thou planted in the sea; and it should obey you” (17:6). The verse is put as a condition: “If ye had.” Luke gives no further word from Jesus about the request before the story begins.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Jesus’ ministry in the Gospels is a ministry in which He says He was sent to preach the kingdom. In Luke He says, “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43), and in Matthew He preached, “Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). Luke 17:7–10 does not use the word kingdom and does not begin “the kingdom is like.” The passage is read as spoken in that ministry and not as a saying that floats free of it. Luke says that when the time came Jesus “stedfastly set his face to go to Jerusalem” (9:51), and the journey is still going on at 17:11.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Luke gives no reason for the story. He gives what stands before it, a run of sayings spoken to the disciples and a request from the apostles, and what stands after it, a notice of the journey and the meeting with ten lepers (17:11–19). Why the story stands where it does is not stated, and no reason is supplied here.'))
A(('tbl', 'The verses that set the scene are laid out below.', [
    ['Luke 17:1', '“Then said he unto the disciples”', 'The company Luke names, and the first saying.'],
    ['Luke 17:3–4', '“Take heed to yourselves: If thy brother trespass against thee, rebuke him”', 'The forgiveness sayings that stand before the story.'],
    ['Luke 17:5', '“Increase our faith”', 'The request of the apostles.'],
    ['Luke 17:6', '“If ye had faith as a grain of mustard seed”', 'The Lord’s answer, put as a condition.'],
    ['Luke 17:7', '“But which of you, having a servant”', 'The story opens with “But” and a question.'],
    ['Luke 4:43', '“I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also”', 'What Jesus says He was sent to do.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The passage is read here in the order Jesus gives it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The first question (17:7). “But which of you, having a servant plowing or feeding cattle, will say unto him by and by, when he is come from the field, Go and sit down to meat?” The story begins with a question put to the hearers, “which of you.” It names a man who has a servant, and it names what the servant has been doing, plowing or feeding cattle. The servant then “is come from the field.” The question asks whether the man will say to him, “by and by,” “Go and sit down to meat.”'))
A(('p', '3.2 The second question (17:8). “And will not rather say unto him, Make ready wherewith I may sup, and gird thyself, and serve me, till I have eaten and drunken; and afterward thou shalt eat and drink?” The second question turns on the words “will not rather say.” It gives the man’s words in four parts: make ready wherewith he may sup; gird thyself; serve me, until he has eaten and drunken; and afterward the servant is to eat and drink.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The third question (17:9). “Doth he thank that servant because he did the things that were commanded him? I trow not.” The third question asks whether the man thanks the servant “because he did the things that were commanded him.” It is followed in the same verse by a short answer, “I trow not.” The text gives these words after the question and does not name the speaker of the answer inside the story. They are set out in Section 4 with Jesus’ other words.'))
A(('p', '3.4 What the story says and does not say. The text says there is a man who has a servant, that the servant has been plowing or feeding cattle, and that he comes from the field. It says the man’s words are “Make ready,” “gird thyself,” “serve me,” and that the servant eats and drinks “afterward.” It says the servant did “the things that were commanded him.” It does not say who the man is, who gave the commands, how long the servant worked, or what the meal is. It does not say whether the servant is paid, whether he is willing, or what the servant says or does when he is told. It does not say that the man is unkind or that the servant is wronged.'))
A(('ctbl', 'The steps in the story are laid out in order.', [
    ['Step', 'What the text says', 'Verse'],
    ['The servant at work', '“having a servant plowing or feeding cattle”', 'Luke 17:7'],
    ['He comes in', '“when he is come from the field”', 'Luke 17:7'],
    ['The first question', '“Go and sit down to meat?”', 'Luke 17:7'],
    ['The second question', '“Make ready wherewith I may sup, and gird thyself, and serve me”', 'Luke 17:8'],
    ['The order of the meals', '“till I have eaten and drunken; and afterward thou shalt eat and drink”', 'Luke 17:8'],
    ['The third question', '“Doth he thank that servant because he did the things that were commanded him?”', 'Luke 17:9'],
    ['The short answer', '“I trow not.”', 'Luke 17:9'],
], [0.24, 0.58, 0.18]))
A(('p', '3.5 What follows the story (17:10). The next verse does not continue the story. It turns to the hearers: “So likewise ye, when ye shall have done all those things which are commanded you, say, We are unprofitable servants: we have done that which was our duty to do.” These are Jesus’ own words, and they are set out word for word in Section 4, where Jesus’ identifications are recorded. They are not paraphrased here.'))
A(('p', '3.6 What follows in Luke (17:11). The next verse begins a new notice: “And it came to pass, as he went to Jerusalem, that he passed through the midst of Samaria and Galilee.” Luke then gives the meeting with ten lepers (17:12–19). Luke does not join them to the story of the servant.'))
A(('p', '3.7 What the passage leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who the man is, who the servant is, what the field is, what the cattle are, what the meal is, or what the commands are. He does not say that the hearers are the servant, and He does not say that the man is any person or being. Each part is read as it stands.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 What Jesus says after the story. Jesus gives one saying of His own after the three questions: “So likewise ye, when ye shall have done all those things which are commanded you, say, We are unprofitable servants: we have done that which was our duty to do” (Luke 17:10). Everything Jesus identifies in Luke 17:7–10 is in that verse and in the short answer of 17:9, and the table records it in His words.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of what Jesus says', 'What Jesus says', 'Verse'],
    ['How He turns to the hearers', '“So likewise ye”', 'Luke 17:10'],
    ['When they are to say it', '“when ye shall have done all those things which are commanded you”', 'Luke 17:10'],
    ['What they are to say', '“We are unprofitable servants”', 'Luke 17:10'],
    ['The reason He gives them to say', '“we have done that which was our duty to do”', 'Luke 17:10'],
    ['The short answer to the third question', '“I trow not.”', 'Luke 17:9'],
], [0.34, 0.46, 0.20]))
A(('p', '4.2 What the words record. In 17:10 Jesus names the hearers by the word “ye,” names a time, “when ye shall have done all those things which are commanded you,” gives them words to say, and gives a reason, “we have done that which was our duty to do.” The word “servants” in 17:10 is in the words He tells them to say. Luke’s text does not say that Jesus calls the man of the story by any name. The short answer in 17:9, “I trow not,” follows the question of 17:9 and is recorded as Luke gives it.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not name. Jesus does not identify the man who has the servant. Jesus does not identify the servant who plows or feeds cattle. Jesus does not identify the field or the cattle. Jesus does not identify the meal, the supper or the girding. Jesus does not identify who gave the commands of 17:9 and 17:10, and He does not say what “all those things which are commanded you” are. Jesus does not state in so many words that the man of the story stands for anyone, or that the servant of the story is the same as the “ye” of 17:10. He says “likewise,” and then speaks to the hearers.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus names only the hearers (“ye”), the time (“when ye shall have done all those things which are commanded you”), the words to say and the reason given, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the man is a certain person, that the servant is a certain company, that the field is a certain place or that the commands are certain commands is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 The explanation stays with its story. The words of 17:10 belong to this passage. The same English word, “servants,” stands in other places in Luke and in the Gospels, and the words of those places do not supply what 17:10 leaves out. The words “unprofitable servant” also stand in Matthew 25:30; they are spoken in another story to another company and are not joined to 17:10.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It repeats none of Jesus’ identifications from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. In Luke the comparison is put as a run of questions to the hearers: which of them, having a servant come in from the field, will tell him to sit down to meat? Will he not rather tell him to make ready and serve, and afterward eat and drink? Does he thank the servant because he did what was commanded? Luke does not open it with “the kingdom is like,” and the meaning is in the movement of the story: a day of work, a further charge when the servant comes in, and no thanks given for what was commanded.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The charge continues.** The servant who has come from the field is not told to sit down. He is told to make ready and to serve until the man has eaten and drunken.',
    '**His own meal comes after.** The order in the story is the man’s supper first and the servant’s eating and drinking “afterward.”',
    '**Thanks is not given for what was commanded.** The question of 17:9 asks whether the man thanks the servant because he did the things that were commanded, and the answer is “I trow not.”',
    '**Doing what is commanded is the duty.** Jesus gives the same reason for the words He tells the hearers to say, “we have done that which was our duty to do.”',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers Luke names before the story are the disciples (17:1) and the apostles who asked the Lord to increase their faith (17:5). They were meant to understand, from the comparison, that a servant who has done what was commanded has done what was his duty, and that the doing of it does not by itself place the one who commanded under thanks to him. These are words spoken in the Kingdom ministry that Jesus was sent to carry out, and they are not words about the church of the Body.'))
A(('p', '5.4 What this section does not do. It does not say who the man or the servant is, or what the field, the cattle, the meal or the commands are. It does not say that the story is the Lord’s answer to the request of 17:5. It does not turn doing what is commanded, serving or being unprofitable into a step by which a person is saved, and it does not make a doctrine of reward, of merit or of the lack of either. It draws no meaning from another parable into this passage.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The passage belongs to Luke 17, and it is kept apart here from three neighbors that also speak of servants or of a man’s response.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 12:42–48', '“Who then is that faithful and wise steward”', 'The Faithful and Unfaithful Servant. A steward set over a household and a master who comes. It has its own setting after Peter’s question and its own study. Luke 17:7–10 is not read into it, and it is not read into Luke 17:7–10.'],
    ['Matt 25:14–30', '“For the kingdom of heaven is as a man travelling into a far country”', 'The Talents. A man who travels and gives goods to servants, with the word “unprofitable” in 25:30. It is told in Matthew to another company and has its own study.'],
    ['Luke 18:9–14', '“trusted in themselves that they were righteous”', 'The Pharisee and the Tax Collector. A story told to “certain” hearers about two men who went up to the temple to pray. It is not a story of a servant or of a master’s command.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. Luke 17:7–10 follows the apostles’ request and the sycamine tree, and it is followed by a notice of the journey and ten lepers (17:11–19). The Faithful and Unfaithful Servant is told in Luke 12 after Peter asks, “Lord, speakest thou this parable unto us, or even to all?” (12:41), and the Talents stands in Matthew 25 after the sayings that begin at Matthew 24. The Pharisee and the Tax Collector begins at Luke 18:9 and is told to “certain” who trusted in themselves. Each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The passage has a man who has a servant, the servant, and the hearers called “ye.” The Faithful and Unfaithful Servant has a steward, a lord and the household, and in its other part a servant who knew his lord’s will. The Talents has a man travelling, three servants and goods committed to them. The Pharisee and the Tax Collector has two men and a temple. No character is carried from one account to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The passage moves through three questions and ends in words Jesus tells the hearers to say. The Faithful and Unfaithful Servant moves from a steward to his lord’s coming. The Talents moves from goods given to a reckoning. The Pharisee and the Tax Collector moves from two prayers to a statement about which man went down to his house justified. None of those movements is read into this passage.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the words “unprofitable servants” in Luke 17:10 are words Jesus tells the hearers to say, “when ye shall have done all those things which are commanded you,” and “the unprofitable servant” in Matthew 25:30 is a servant cast into outer darkness. The same English word stands in two stories with two speakers and two settings, and neither verse is used to explain the other. Second, the master in Luke 12:37 “shall gird himself, and make them to sit down to meat, and will come forth and serve them,” and the man in Luke 17:8 says “gird thyself, and serve me.” They are different stories with different words, and neither is used to explain the other. Third, the “righteous” of Luke 18:9 are not the “ye” of Luke 17:10, and nothing in either passage joins them.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. In Luke 17 Jesus speaks to the disciples (17:1), and the apostles speak to Him (17:5). The passage belongs to the earthly ministry in which He said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43) and preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17), and to the journey to Jerusalem that was under way (Luke 9:51; 17:11).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The servant and the man were told of before any of that, and they are not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Luke 17:7–10 speaks of the Body of Christ, and the passage is not read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The servant, the commands, the supper and the words “We are unprofitable servants” are not a description of the Body of Christ. The passage is not read as a universal statement of what a person must say or do to be received by God. The doing of what is commanded and the saying of what Jesus tells the hearers to say are actions in a story and in a saying, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The words are spoken in His earthly ministry, and they are not carried over as a statement the Body must make. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this passage: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9). The passage says nothing of reward or loss for the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the comparison shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of humility.** The servant has done what was commanded, and the story gives no thanks for it. A reader learns to hold his own service without counting it as a claim on others.',
    '**A lesson of steadiness.** The servant goes from the field to the further charge of the table. A reader learns to carry on with the duty in front of him when one task is done.',
    '**A lesson of order.** In the story the man’s supper comes first and the servant’s afterward. A reader learns to notice the order of what he is charged to do.',
    '**A lesson of plain speech.** Jesus gives the hearers the words, “we have done that which was our duty to do.” A reader learns to say what he has done without adding to it.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the servant, that the Body is the man, or that the Body is the company called “ye.” They are principles drawn from what the passage shows.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus asks which of the hearers, having a servant plowing or feeding cattle, will say to him when he comes from the field, “Go and sit down to meat.” He asks whether the man will not rather say, “Make ready wherewith I may sup, and gird thyself, and serve me, till I have eaten and drunken; and afterward thou shalt eat and drink.” He asks whether the man thanks that servant because he did the things that were commanded him, and the short answer follows, “I trow not.” Jesus then says to the hearers, “So likewise ye, when ye shall have done all those things which are commanded you, say, We are unprofitable servants: we have done that which was our duty to do.” Luke places these words after the apostles’ request, “Increase our faith,” and the saying about the sycamine tree, and before the notice of the journey in 17:11. Jesus names the hearers (“ye”), a time, the words to say and the reason given, and nothing else in the passage. He spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the man or the servant is, or that makes doing what is commanded, or saying “unprofitable,” a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a servant at work, a servant come in, a charge to make ready and serve, no thanks for what was commanded, and words Jesus gives the hearers to say.',
    '**The meaning:** what is commanded and done is duty, and the doing of it is not given thanks as though the one who commanded were in debt.',
    '**The boundaries:** only the hearers, the time, the words and the reason in 17:10 are named; the man, the servant, the field, the meal and the commands are not.',
    '**The present lesson:** humility, steadiness, order and plain speech, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

